from expand_common import *
f=family; F=Fraction
t='p_types'
f(t,'unsigned-range','A width-explicit unsigned range',r'An unsigned representation has $n+3$ bits with no padding. Find its maximum representable value.',r'$2^{n+3}-1$',[r'$2^{n+3}$',r'$2^{n+2}-1$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:2**(n+3)-1,[lambda n:2**(n+3),lambda n:2**(n+2)-1,lambda n:2**n],r'There are two to the width distinct bit patterns. Starting at zero, the last represented integer is one less than the pattern count. All bits equal one attain that value; the next power requires another bit position.','A declared type size includes representation assumptions and must not be guessed from its name.',['Every language fixes every integer type to the same width.','An unsigned representation includes negative values.','The number of values equals the maximum value.'],'A range uses value bits, while storage can also include padding or platform-specific widths. This question states the no-padding width explicitly. Counting values and counting the maximum differ by one because zero belongs to the range.')
f(t,'unsigned-addition','A modular register addition with a carry out',r'In an unsigned $(n+3)$-bit modular register, add $n+1$ to the maximum value. Find the stored result.',r'$n$',[r'$n+1$',r'$2^{n+3}+n$',r'$-n$'],lambda n:n,[lambda n:n+1,lambda n:2**(n+3)+n,lambda n:-n],r'The maximum is the modulus minus one. The sum is the modulus plus $n$, and reduction modulo the register modulus leaves $n$. The discarded carry does not make the stored register value negative.','A language expression may undergo promotions before an eventual assignment to a narrow register.',['Every promoted expression is computed directly in its destination width.','Signed overflow has the same language rule as unsigned wraparound.','A carry out is part of the fixed-width stored value.'],'The question specifies a modular-register model, not an unspecified promoted language expression. In C, operand types and promotions determine the evaluation width, and destination conversion happens afterward. Signed overflow must be evaluated under its own language rules.')
f(t,'signed-minimum','A representation-specific negative endpoint',r'A two’s complement signed representation has $n$ value-and-sign bits with no padding. Find its minimum value.',r'$-2^{n-1}$',[r'$-2^n$',r'$-(2^{n-1}-1)$',r'$0$'],lambda n:-2**(n-1),[lambda n:-2**n,lambda n:-(2**(n-1)-1),lambda n:0],r'The most significant bit has negative weight equal to half the full unsigned modulus, while all lower bits have positive weights. Setting only that high bit gives the minimum; adding any lower one-bit increases the value.','The minimum has no positive counterpart in the same signed range.',['The absolute value of every representable signed value fits the same signed type.','The signed range is perfectly symmetric.','The high bit has the same positive weight as in unsigned decoding.'],'The maximum is one less than the magnitude of the minimum. Negating the minimum therefore lies outside the range. This endpoint asymmetry is important for conversions, absolute-value operations and overflow reasoning.')
f(t,'alignment','Padding under an explicitly specified layout',r'A record has a first member occupying $n$ bytes, followed by a four-byte member requiring four-byte alignment. The record also requires four-byte alignment. Find total size under this layout.',r'$4\lceil n/4\rceil+4$',[r'$n+4$',r'$4\lceil n/4\rceil$',r'$n+8$'],lambda n:4*((n+3)//4)+4,[lambda n:n+4,lambda n:4*((n+3)//4),lambda n:n+8],r'Round the first member end up to the next four-byte boundary before placing the second member. Add the four occupied bytes. The resulting end is already aligned for the record, so it needs no additional rounding.','Padding rules depend on the stated layout and cannot be inferred from member widths alone.',['A record size always equals the sum of member widths.','Alignment removes occupied bytes.','The record may end before its last member ends.'],'Alignment constraints can insert unused bytes between members or at the end of a record. The exact layout here is specified, so the offset and final size are determined. Other platforms, packing rules or member orders require recalculation.',seeds=(5,9))
f(t,'array-extent','Actual array storage rather than a decayed pointer',r'A real two-dimensional array has $n$ rows and $n+1$ columns, with four-byte elements and no padding between elements. Find its total byte size.',r'$4n(n+1)$',[r'$4n$',r'$4(n+1)$',r'$n(n+1)$'],lambda n:4*n*(n+1),[lambda n:4*n,lambda n:4*(n+1),lambda n:n*(n+1)],r'Multiply row count by column count to obtain the element count, then multiply by the specified element size. An actual array occupies every element, unlike a pointer value referring to the array. Row stride is the column count times element size.','An array parameter adjusted to a pointer does not retain the full array size in sizeof.',['Every array expression always remains an array under every operation.','The number of rows alone determines storage.','A pointer object occupies all elements of the target array.'],'In contexts that adjust or decay an array to a pointer, operations inspect the pointer representation rather than the whole target allocation. The stated actual array object avoids that ambiguity. Storage size, row stride and pointer size are separate quantities.')
f(t,'packed-bits','Ceiling division for an explicit packed format',r'A serialized format stores $n$ Boolean flags in consecutive bits of eight-bit bytes, using the minimum whole-byte count. Find the byte count.',r'$\lceil n/8\rceil$',[r'$n$',r'$8n$',r'$\lceil n/8\rceil+1$'],lambda n:(n+7)//8,[lambda n:n,lambda n:8*n,lambda n:(n+7)//8+1],r'A full byte holds eight flags, and any nonempty remainder needs one additional byte. Ceiling division expresses both cases without wasting a byte when the count is an exact multiple. The serialized bit-packing rule is explicitly part of the model.','This format rule does not establish a portable in-memory C bit-field layout.',['Every C bit-field is serialized in the same order on every platform.','A partial final byte can be omitted.','A packed-bit format is the same as an array of one-byte flags.'],'Serialization fixes a representation independently of implementation-defined object layout. C bit-field ordering, allocation units and padding need their own platform assumptions. The minimum-byte count follows from the explicit consecutive-bit format rather than a language-level layout guess.',seeds=(9,17))
f(t,'floating-integers','The endpoint of consecutive integer exactness',r'A binary floating representation has $n$ significant bits including its leading bit, with enough exponent range. Find the largest nonnegative integer up to which all consecutive integers are exact.',r'$2^n$',[r'$2^n-1$',r'$2^{n+1}$',r'$n$'],lambda n:2**n,[lambda n:2**n-1,lambda n:2**(n+1),lambda n:n],r'Every integer below the next power needs at most $n$ significant binary bits. The power itself is exact with a one-bit significand. Immediately above it, spacing doubles to two, so the next odd integer cannot be represented exactly.','Beyond this endpoint some larger integers are still exact, but not every consecutive integer.',['No integer larger than the endpoint is representable.','Floating values are uniformly spaced across all magnitudes.','Enough exponent range guarantees unlimited integer precision.'],'Precision governs the spacing within a binade, while exponent range governs reachable magnitudes. Large powers of two remain exact, and some larger even integers are representable. The endpoint concerns consecutive exactness, not the largest finite value.')
f(t,'negative-division','Truncation toward zero versus floor',r'Using C17 signed integer division, divide $-(3n+1)$ by 3; all values fit the chosen signed type. Find the quotient.',r'$-n$',[r'$-n-1$',r'$n$',r'$-3n-1$'],lambda n:-n,[lambda n:-n-1,lambda n:n,lambda n:-3*n-1],r'The real quotient is minus the integer $n$ minus one third. Truncating toward zero removes the fractional part toward the less negative integer, giving $-n$. A floor operation would move to the next more negative integer instead.','The remainder reconstructs the dividend with the truncated quotient.',['C17 signed integer division always takes the mathematical floor.','A negative dividend always has a nonnegative remainder.','Overflow constraints can be ignored for every signed division.'],'The identity dividend equals quotient times divisor plus remainder yields remainder minus one here. The question excludes representability problems explicitly; other inputs, including dividing the minimum signed value by minus one, need an additional range check.')

t='p_flow'
f(t,'ordered-updates','An accumulator before the increment',r'With mathematical integer arithmetic, execute `i=0; s=0; while(i<n){ s+=i; ++i; }`. Find final `s`.',r'$n(n-1)/2$',[r'$n(n+1)/2$',r'$n^2$',r'$n$'],lambda n:n*(n-1)//2,[lambda n:n*(n+1)//2,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n],r'The added values are zero through $n-1$. Before the iteration with index $i$, the accumulator contains the sum of smaller indices. The update adds the current index, and only afterward does the increment advance the checkpoint.','Reordering the increment and addition changes the accumulated sequence.',['The two update orders always produce identical results.','The loop includes the index equal to its upper bound.','The accumulator itself must decrease to prove termination.'],'An invariant is tied to a program location. Adding after increment instead sums one through the bound. The remaining index distance is the decreasing ranking, while the accumulator relation proves correctness; these two obligations must be kept separate.')
f(t,'short-circuit','A skipped side effect at the final guard',r'For representable nonnegative integers, execute `i=0; j=0; while(i<n && ++j){ ++i; }`. Find final `j`.',r'$n$',[r'$n+1$',r'$n-1$',r'$2n$'],lambda n:n,[lambda n:n+1,lambda n:n-1,lambda n:2*n],r'The left operand is evaluated first. During each of the $n$ successful guards it is true, so the incremented second operand is positive and the body runs. At the final guard the left operand is false, so the second operand and its side effect are skipped.','Replacing logical AND by a bitwise operation can change both values and side-effect evaluation.',['Logical AND evaluates both operands unconditionally.','The final false guard increments the second operand.','Every operator sequences side effects in the same way.'],'Short-circuiting provides conditional evaluation and a defined ordering between these logical operands. A bitwise operation evaluates both operands and does not share that rule. Any rewrite with side effects must preserve evaluation, sequencing and integer-value semantics.')
f(t,'continue-for','Counting the body of a filtered for loop',r'Execute `s=0; for(i=1;i<=3*n;++i){ if(i%3==0) continue; ++s; }` with all integer operations representable. Find final `s`.',r'$2n$',[r'$3n$',r'$n$',r'$2n-1$'],lambda n:2*n,[lambda n:3*n,lambda n:n,lambda n:2*n-1],r'The update expression executes after both ordinary iterations and continue paths in this for loop. Thus every index in the full range is visited. Exactly one index per block of three skips the increment of the separate accumulator.','Moving the index update into a later while-loop body can make a continue path nonterminating.',['Continue exits the entire for loop.','Continue skips the for-loop update expression.','The source of an update is irrelevant to control-flow correctness.'],'A continue targets the loop continuation point. In the stated for loop that point runs its update, but a manual increment after a continue in a while body would not execute. The apparent loop rewrite therefore needs a path-by-path sequencing check.')
f(t,'break-checkpoint','An exact threshold reached at the body checkpoint',r'Add positive indices starting at one and break immediately after the running sum first reaches $n(n+1)/2$. Find the number of additions.',r'$n$',[r'$n-1$',r'$n+1$',r'$2n$'],lambda n:n,[lambda n:n-1,lambda n:n+1,lambda n:2*n],r'After $k$ additions, the sum is $k(k+1)/2$. This expression strictly increases for nonnegative indices. It equals the given threshold at $k=n$, and all earlier sums are smaller, so the first qualifying body checkpoint is exactly that one.','Testing the threshold before versus after the addition changes which operations have already occurred.',['A break always executes the remaining statements in its body.','The first threshold crossing may be counted without identifying the test checkpoint.','The monotonicity argument does not need the positive increment premise.'],'The test location determines whether the last increment is included. Break exits immediately rather than executing the remaining body. Positivity supplies strict monotonicity, which proves that equality at the specified index is the first threshold crossing.')
f(t,'call-tree','Counting calls rather than active stack frames',r'A recursive routine at parameter $k>0$ calls itself twice at parameter $k-1$; a zero-parameter call returns without children. Starting at $n$, count all calls.',r'$2^{n+1}-1$',[r'$2^n$',r'$2^{n+1}$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:2**(n+1)-1,[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:2**(n+1),lambda n:n+1],r'The execution tree has two to the depth nodes at each level, including the root and zero-parameter leaves. Sum those levels through depth $n$. A depth-first call stack contains one path at a time, not every previously executed call.','Exponential call count can coexist with linear active stack depth.',['The stack stores the entire recursion tree simultaneously in every ordinary execution.','The base calls do not count as function calls.','Two children imply exactly two calls in total.'],'Ordinary sequential depth-first recursion completes one subtree before traversing the next. Active frames therefore follow a root-to-leaf path, while total work counts all paths. Base calls also execute the condition and return, so they belong to the total call count.')
f(t,'recursive-local-work','A linear recursion with a growing local loop',r'A routine at parameter $k>0$ performs exactly $k$ body operations, then calls itself once at $k-1$; the zero case performs none. Starting at $n$, count body operations.',r'$n(n+1)/2$',[r'$n$',r'$n^2$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:n*(n+1)//2,[lambda n:n,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:2**n],r'The recursive parameters visit every positive integer from the initial value down to one. Sum the local loop lengths over these calls. One child gives linear call count but does not make the local work constant.','Tail position does not eliminate work already performed in a local loop.',['A tail-recursive function always has constant total time.','One recursive child means there are no local operations.','The cost of every recursive call is the cost of its final base case.'],'Tail-call optimization, when available, can change active stack usage, not erase previous arithmetic. Total cost must include each call local toll. Here the decreasing argument proves termination and the toll sum explains why work is quadratic despite a linear-depth path.')
f(t,'sequential-swap','Reasoning with the updated value in each statement',r'With exact representable integer intermediates, start `a=n; b=2;` and execute `a=a+b; b=a-b; a=a-b;`. Find final `a`.',r'$2$',[r'$n$',r'$n+2$',r'$n-2$'],lambda n:2,[lambda n:n,lambda n:n+2,lambda n:n-2],r'After the first statement the values are $(n+2,2)$. The second uses the updated first value and produces $(n+2,n)$. The third uses this updated second value and produces $(2,n)$, completing the swap.','Exact intermediate representability is part of the stated arithmetic-swap model.',['Each statement always reads only the initial values.','Signed intermediate overflow is harmless because the final values fit.','Sequential assignment is the same as simultaneous tuple assignment.'],'The proof follows the actual execution order. A signed-language overflow at an intermediate addition can invalidate the implementation even when both final values would fit. A temporary-variable swap avoids relying on arithmetic range beyond the original values.')
f(t,'do-while','A body executed before its first guard',r'Using signed integers with enough range, execute `i=n; do { --i; } while(i>=0);`. Count body executions.',r'$n+1$',[r'$n$',r'$n-1$',r'$2n$'],lambda n:n+1,[lambda n:n,lambda n:n-1,lambda n:2*n],r'The body changes the index successively to $n-1$ down through zero and then minus one. The guard becomes false only after that final decrement. A do-while executes before testing, so its endpoint count differs from some superficially similar while loops.','Unsigned indices make a nonnegative comparison permanently true.',['Every integer type permits a negative terminal value.','Do-while tests its guard before every body including the first.','Replacing a signed counter by unsigned never changes termination.'],'The signed terminal minus-one value makes the stated condition false. An unsigned counter instead wraps and remains nonnegative, so that condition cannot terminate the loop. Control-flow correctness depends on both guard placement and type semantics.')

t='g_number'
f(t,'biased-exponent','Decoding a normal biased exponent',r'Assume $n\ge4$. A format uses $n$ exponent bits with bias $2^{n-1}-1$. A normal value has encoded exponent $n-3$. Find the unbiased exponent.',r'$n-2-2^{n-1}$',[r'$n-3$',r'$n-4+2^{n-1}$',r'$n-3-2^{n-1}$'],lambda n:n-2-2**(n-1),[lambda n:n-3,lambda n:n-4+2**(n-1),lambda n:n-3-2**(n-1)],r'Normal decoding subtracts the bias from the encoded exponent. Preserve the minus-one component of the bias when opening the subtraction. The encoded value is nonzero and not all ones in the chosen cases, so the normal decoding rule applies.','Reserved exponent patterns require separate decoding rules.',['The normal bias subtraction decodes every special pattern.','The sign bit is part of the exponent bias.','The encoded exponent is already the mathematical exponent.'],'Formats can assign the zero and all-ones exponent patterns to subnormals, zeros, infinities or NaNs. Applying the normal rule to those patterns would ignore the special-value convention. The question explicitly selects a normal encoded exponent.')
f(t,'fixed-point','A fractional bit with a known scaling factor',r'A nonnegative fixed-point representation has $n$ fractional bits. Its stored unsigned integer is $3\cdot2^n+1$. Find the represented real value.',r'$3+2^{-n}$',[r'$3+1/n$',r'$3\cdot2^n+1$',r'$3-2^{-n}$'],lambda n:3+F(1,2**n),[lambda n:3+F(1,n),lambda n:3*2**n+1,lambda n:3-F(1,2**n)],r'Every stored integer unit has real weight two to minus the fractional-bit count. Divide the stored value by $2^n$: the high part becomes three and the low one-bit becomes a positive fractional increment. The scale is not a divisor equal to the bit count.','A fixed-point step is uniform within its specified representation.',['Fixed-point spacing changes at every power of two like normal floating point.','Increasing fractional precision leaves the step unchanged.','The stored integer and represented real number are identical.'],'The common scale factor makes every neighboring stored integer differ by the same real increment. Changing the number of fractional bits changes that scale. Floating formats instead combine significand and exponent and can have magnitude-dependent spacing.')
f(t,'negative-decode','One word under two integer interpretations',r'Assume $n\ge3$. An $n$-bit word has unsigned value $2^n-3$. Decode it as two’s complement signed.',r'$-3$',[r'$2^n-3$',r'$3$',r'$-2^{n-1}$'],lambda n:-3,[lambda n:2**n-3,lambda n:3,lambda n:-2**(n-1)],r'The unsigned value lies in the upper half of the word range, so its sign bit is one. Signed decoding subtracts the full modulus $2^n$, leaving minus three. The bit pattern is unchanged; only its interpretation changes.','Signedness changes interpretation, not the stored bit pattern.',['The word must physically change before being read as signed.','A high sign bit always represents the minimum value.','Every unsigned value has the same numeric signed interpretation.'],'Two’s complement gives the high bit negative weight. The same pattern can therefore represent different numbers under unsigned and signed decoding. Only the single-high-bit word gives the minimum; lower one-bits raise its signed value.')
f(t,'overflow-word','Stored hardware result versus mathematical signed sum',r'Assume $n\ge2$. Add two maximum $n$-bit signed two’s complement positive words in a modulo-$2^n$ hardware adder. Decode the stored output word as signed.',r'$-2$',[r'$2^n-2$',r'$2^{n-1}-1$',r'$0$'],lambda n:-2,[lambda n:2**n-2,lambda n:2**(n-1)-1,lambda n:0],r'Each operand has mathematical value $2^{n-1}-1$. Their sum is $2^n-2$, which is a valid unsigned output pattern but outside the signed positive range. Signed decoding of that upper-half pattern subtracts $2^n$ and gives minus two.','Hardware wrap does not establish defined signed-overflow behavior for a source-language expression.',['A correct carry bit always proves signed addition stayed in range.','Every C signed addition wraps by definition.','A representable output pattern means the mathematical signed sum fit.'],'A hardware word exists for every modular sum, but signed overflow is a mismatch between that word interpretation and the mathematical sum. Language rules can declare such an operation undefined. The explicit hardware model avoids silently claiming a source-language guarantee.')
f(t,'ones-zero','Distinct encodings of numerical zero',r'In an $n$-bit one’s complement signed format, count distinct bit patterns representing numerical zero.',r'$2$',[r'$1$',r'$n$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:2,[lambda n:1,lambda n:n,lambda n:2**n],r'The all-zero pattern represents positive zero. Complementing it gives the all-one pattern representing negative zero. These are distinct encodings of the same numerical value under one’s complement interpretation; they must not be confused with two’s complement encoding.','Counting encodings is different from counting distinct numerical values.',['Two zero patterns represent two distinct mathematical integers.','One-complement and two’s complement have the same zero encoding count.','Every pattern represents zero because sign can be ignored.'],'A representation can be many-to-one at numerical zero. That affects comparison, normalization and arithmetic conventions even though mathematics has one integer zero. Two-complement eliminates this duplicated-zero encoding and allocates the extra pattern to another negative value.')
f(t,'finite-fraction','A repeating-looking but finite binary fraction',r'A binary fraction contains exactly $n$ ones after its radix point and then stops. Find its exact value.',r'$1-2^{-n}$',[r'$1$',r'$2^{-n}$',r'$1-2^{1-n}$'],lambda n:1-F(1,2**n),[lambda n:1,lambda n:F(1,2**n),lambda n:1-F(1,2**(n-1))],r'The bit weights are one half through two to minus the final position. Their finite geometric sum leaves a gap of $2^{-n}$ below one. The infinite all-one fraction reaches one, but that limiting identity is not the finite value.','A finite representation and its infinite extension can differ by one least-position unit.',['A finite all-one fraction always equals one.','Each fractional bit has the same weight.','The final one-bit can be omitted without changing the exact value.'],'Every fractional position halves the preceding weight. Keeping the endpoint distinguishes the finite geometric sum from its infinite limit. An exact decoding question requires the actual stored length, not an informal repeating visual pattern.')
f(t,'floating-spacing','The spacing inside a specified binade',r'A binary normal floating format has five significant bits including its leading bit. Within the binade starting at $2^n$, find the spacing between adjacent values, assuming exponent range is sufficient.',r'$2^{n-4}$',[r'$2^{n-5}$',r'$2^{-n}$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:2**(n-4),[lambda n:2**(n-5),lambda n:F(1,2**n),lambda n:2**n],r'Five significant bits give four fractional positions after the leading one. The least significand increment is $2^{-4}$, scaled by the binade exponent factor $2^n$. Multiply the weights; the leading bit counts toward the precision.','Spacing changes at a binade boundary.',['Floating spacing is the same at every exponent.','Five significant bits always means five fraction bits.','Exponent range alone determines local precision.'],'Normalized significands have a fixed number of fractional positions, but the exponent scales all their increments. At the next binade that scale doubles. Exponent reach and significand precision are separate format properties with different numerical consequences.')
f(t,'hex-width','A radix conversion without magnitude confusion',r'A fixed-width word has $n$ hexadecimal digits, retaining leading zeros. Find the number of binary bits needed to store every possible word.',r'$4n$',[r'$n$',r'$16n$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:4*n,[lambda n:n,lambda n:16*n,lambda n:2**n],r'A hexadecimal digit has sixteen possibilities, exactly matching four binary bits. Independent digits give sixteen to the digit-count patterns, or two to four times that count bits. Retaining width and leading zeros fixes the representation length.','Minimal length of one particular value can be shorter than the width of the format.',['Every hexadecimal digit contributes sixteen binary bits.','Leading zeros change the numeric value.','The largest representable value equals the number of patterns.'],'The format must distinguish all possible words, including high leading digits. A specific small value may have a shorter minimal notation, but that does not reduce the stipulated storage width. Pattern count includes zero and therefore exceeds the largest unsigned value by one.')

t='g_boolean'
f(t,'implication-chain','Truth assignments forced into a monotone pattern',r'A Boolean function is true exactly when $x_1\Rightarrow x_2,\ldots,x_{n-1}\Rightarrow x_n$ all hold. Count its true rows.',r'$n+1$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n$',r'$2^{n-1}$'],lambda n:n+1,[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n,lambda n:2**(n-1)],r'A true value cannot be followed by a false value anywhere along the chain. Therefore every legal row consists of an initial block of zeros followed by a block of ones. Choose the transition position, including all-zero and all-one endpoints.','An implication chain is weaker than requiring every neighboring pair to be equivalent.',['The chain forces all bits equal.','The all-zero row violates every implication.','There may be several transitions from one back to zero.'],'The material implication forbids only a local one-to-zero pattern. Equality would also forbid zero-to-one transitions and leave only two constant rows. The chain permits exactly one increasing transition, whose possible location determines the count.')
f(t,'parity-table','The truth-table size of a parity function',r'Count true rows of $x_1\oplus\cdots\oplus x_n$.',r'$2^{n-1}$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n$',r'$2^{n-1}-1$'],lambda n:2**(n-1),[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n,lambda n:2**(n-1)-1],r'Flipping one input reverses parity and is its own inverse. It pairs each false row with exactly one true row, so exactly half the rows are true. This proves the count without assuming parity classes are equally large merely from their names.','The pairing must be a bijection that actually changes the predicate.',['Every named pair of categories is automatically equally sized.','Flipping a parity input leaves the output unchanged.','The input rows with all equal bits are the only parity rows.'],'A valid pairing explains equal class sizes, rather than presupposing them. XOR changes when exactly one input flips, so the involution maps between the two classes and has no fixed rows. Other weighted symbol-count problems may lack this symmetry.')
f(t,'self-dual','Choosing one output per complementary input pair',r'Count Boolean functions on $n$ inputs satisfying $F(\overline x)=\overline{F(x)}$ for every input word.',r'$2^{2^{n-1}}$',[r'$2^{2^n}$',r'$2^{n-1}$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:2**(2**(n-1)),[lambda n:2**(2**n),lambda n:2**(n-1),lambda n:2**n],r'Input complement pairs the truth-table rows into disjoint two-element orbits. Choose the output on one representative of each pair freely; the other output is forced opposite. There are $2^{n-1}$ independent representative outputs, not that many functions.','Self-duality is a relation between complementary rows, not the same as output negation alone.',['A self-dual function is identical to its output complement on each fixed input.','Complemented inputs are independent output choices.','Every function is self-dual.'],'The equation compares two different input rows. On one fixed row an output cannot equal its own Boolean complement. The output choices become coupled across pairs, reducing the free truth-table bits by half.',seeds=(3,4))
f(t,'pair-products','A disjunction of disjoint two-input products',r'For $F=\bigvee_{i=1}^{n}(a_i\land b_i)$ with all inputs distinct, count true rows.',r'$4^n-3^n$',[r'$4^n$',r'$3^n$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:4**n-3**n,[lambda n:4**n,lambda n:3**n,lambda n:2**n],r'Each input pair has four possible states, and avoiding its true product excludes only state 11. For the output to be false, all pairs must avoid that state, giving three choices per pair. Subtract the false rows from the full table.','Sharing inputs between product terms destroys the independent-pair count.',['Every product term must be true for the disjunction to be true.','A chosen true product gives a unique representation of every true row.','Products using shared inputs remain independent four-state blocks.'],'A true row can satisfy several product terms, so counting one witness term overcounts it. Distinct input blocks justify the product in the complement count. Shared inputs require conditioning or another technique to handle the resulting dependence.')
f(t,'exact-weight','A symmetric function with an exact Hamming weight',r'A Boolean function on $n$ inputs is true exactly when precisely two input bits are one. Count its minterms.',r'$\binom n2$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n(n-1)$',r'$n^2$'],lambda n:C(n,2),[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n*(n-1),lambda n:n*n],r'Choose the two one-bit positions; every other bit is forced to zero. The positions form an unordered pair, so exchanging their selection order does not create a new input row. Each true row corresponds to one canonical minterm.','Canonical minterms count true rows, not an implementation minimum.',['A minterm corresponds to a false row.','Ordering the two selected positions changes the row.','Minimum gate count always equals canonical minterm count.'],'Canonical minterms explicitly name every input position and enumerate true assignments. A simplified implementation can share structure and need fewer terms or gates. The requested canonical count is determined by rows rather than by a chosen circuit realization.')
f(t,'affine-functions','Free coefficients in an affine Boolean expression',r'Count distinct functions $c\oplus a_1x_1\oplus\cdots\oplus a_nx_n$ with Boolean coefficients.',r'$2^{n+1}$',[r'$2^n$',r'$2^{2^n}$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:2**(n+1),[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:2**(2**n),lambda n:n+1],r'The constant coefficient is recovered by evaluating at zero. Evaluating at each standard unit input then recovers its corresponding linear coefficient. Thus every coefficient choice gives a distinct function, and there are $n+1$ independently chosen bits.','Affine Boolean arithmetic uses XOR, not ordinary integer addition.',['Coefficients can be arbitrary integers without changing the class count.','Every Boolean function is affine.','Removing the constant coefficient leaves the same number of functions.'],'The coefficient operations take place over the two-element field. General Boolean functions may contain higher-degree products and are far more numerous. Omitting the constant restricts the zero-input output and halves the number of coefficient choices.',seeds=(3,4))
f(t,'canonical-false','At-most-one true input and maxterm enumeration',r'A function is true when at most one of its $n$ inputs is one. Count clauses in canonical CNF.',r'$2^n-n-1$',[r'$2^n-1$',r'$n+1$',r'$2^{n-1}$'],lambda n:2**n-n-1,[lambda n:2**n-1,lambda n:n+1,lambda n:2**(n-1)],r'True rows consist of the all-zero row and the $n$ single-one rows. Canonical CNF enumerates all remaining false rows, so subtract those $n+1$ rows from the full table. Confusing true-row count with maxterm count complements the function.','The all-zero row satisfies at-most-one but not exactly-one.',['At-most-one and exactly-one have identical truth tables.','Canonical CNF selects true rows directly.','An input row may create more than one distinct canonical maxterm.'],'A canonical maxterm is false at precisely its matching row and true elsewhere. Its product therefore excludes the function false rows. The all-zero endpoint distinguishes the two natural-language weight restrictions and affects the exact count by one.')
f(t,'quadratic-anf','Counting functions by a unique algebraic normal form',r'Count Boolean functions on $n$ inputs whose algebraic normal form has degree at most two, including a constant term.',r'$2^{1+n+\binom n2}$',[r'$2^{2^n}$',r'$2^{n+\binom n2}$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:2**(1+n+C(n,2)),[lambda n:2**(2**n),lambda n:2**(n+C(n,2)),lambda n:2**n],r'The allowable square-free monomials are the constant, each singleton input, and each pair product. Algebraic normal form has unique coefficients over the two-element field, so independently select the coefficient of every allowed monomial. Monomial count is an exponent, not the function count.','Repeated powers of a Boolean input reduce to that same input.',['A squared Boolean input is a new independent monomial.','Algebraic normal form uses OR as its coefficient addition.','Every degree-two coefficient choice gives the same truth table.'],'On Boolean inputs, powers greater than zero equal the original variable. The normal form therefore uses square-free products and XOR coefficient addition. Uniqueness is what permits converting coefficient choices into a count of distinct functions rather than merely expressions.',seeds=(3,4))
f(t,'symmetric-functions','Truth-table outputs grouped by Hamming weight',r'Count Boolean functions on $n$ inputs whose value depends only on the number of one-bits.',r'$2^{n+1}$',[r'$2^n$',r'$2^{2^n}$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:2**(n+1),[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:2**(2**n),lambda n:n+1],r'The possible Hamming weights are zero through $n$, making $n+1$ distinct input layers. Symmetry forces equal output within each layer but places no relation between different layers. Choose one output bit independently for each layer.','Input symmetry does not imply self-duality.',['Every symmetric function has the same output on complementary weights.','A symmetric function must be either AND or OR.','The weight-zero layer can be omitted.'],'Complementing all inputs changes weight from a value to its complement in the input count. Symmetry alone does not force those two layer outputs to be opposite or equal. Self-duality would add a separate coupling between complementary layers.',seeds=(3,4))

t='g_gates'
f(t,'and-tree','A fixed-fan-in reduction tree',r'A binary-input AND tree combines $2^n$ independent input signals, without sharing or simplification. Count gates.',r'$2^n-1$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n$',r'$n-1$'],lambda n:2**n-1,[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n,lambda n:n-1],r'Each binary gate reduces the number of separate partial signals by one. Reducing all inputs to one output therefore needs one fewer gate than inputs. A balanced tree attains this count and has a different depth measure.','Gate count and critical-path depth are different objectives.',['Balancing always changes the gate count of a binary reduction tree.','The gate count equals its balanced depth.','Unbounded fan-in can be substituted without changing the model.'],'Balancing rearranges the tree but retains the binary-node count. Depth measures the longest dependency path. Allowing gates of arbitrarily large fan-in changes the cost model and can reduce both node count and the depth, so it must be stated explicitly.')
f(t,'nand-and','Restoring polarity after a NAND',r'Build $n$ separate two-input AND outputs using only two-input NAND gates, with tied-input NAND inversion and no complemented inputs or sharing. Count gates in this construction.',r'$2n$',[r'$n$',r'$3n$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:2*n,[lambda n:n,lambda n:3*n,lambda n:n+1],r'For each output, a NAND gives the complement of the required product. A second NAND with its inputs tied inverts that result, giving AND. The stipulated independent outputs do not share these two gates, so multiply the per-output count.','Availability of complemented signals can change a polarity-restoration construction.',['NAND directly produces the uncomplemented AND output.','Tied inputs cannot invert a Boolean signal.','Separate independent outputs may always share their entire computation.'],'A tied-input NAND maps a signal to its complement by idempotence of AND. Signal availability and output sharing affect a circuit-cost question; the given model deliberately excludes those alternative optimizations. A construction count is not an unstated universal lower bound.')
f(t,'xor-chain','An XOR construction priced in NAND gates',r'Combine $n+1$ input bits by a serial XOR chain. Every two-input XOR uses the standard four-NAND realization, with no sharing between XOR units. Count NAND gates.',r'$4n$',[r'$n$',r'$3n$',r'$2n$'],lambda n:4*n,[lambda n:n,lambda n:3*n,lambda n:2*n],r'A serial chain reducing $n+1$ signals uses $n$ two-input XOR units. Substitute the specified four-gate implementation for every unit. This multiplies unit count by implementation cost; it does not turn a NAND gate into an abstract XOR primitive.','Balancing the XOR dependency tree can reduce depth without changing this substituted gate count.',['A serial XOR chain has constant depth.','An abstract XOR and a NAND have the same stipulated cost.','Any shared realization preserves the no-sharing construction count.'],'The Boolean parity function is associative, so regrouping can balance its XOR dependency tree. With the fixed independent four-NAND implementation, unit count stays the same. More aggressive gate sharing would require a new circuit analysis and is excluded here.')
f(t,'mux-tree','A binary selection tree',r'A full tree of two-to-one multiplexers selects among $2^n$ independent data inputs. Count multiplexers.',r'$2^n-1$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n$',r'$2^n-2$'],lambda n:2**n-1,[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n,lambda n:2**n-2],r'The data inputs are tree leaves and every two-to-one selector is a binary internal node. A full binary tree has one fewer internal node than leaves. The selection depth uses the number of address bits, but that is not the number of selector blocks.','Selection polarity and input labeling affect function correctness even when block counts agree.',['Multiplexer input-zero and input-one ports are interchangeable without changing the function.','Selector count always equals select-bit count.','An address bit may be omitted while retaining all independent choices.'],'A multiplexer sends one data input or the other according to its stated select convention. Reversing the port labels complements that local choice and can change the overall selected data word. Structural block counting cannot detect a wrong polarity mapping.')
f(t,'ripple-delay','A final sum path longer than the carry path',r'In an $n$-bit ripple adder, each carry stage takes two delay units and the last sum takes three units after its carry input arrives. Inputs arrive at zero; find the maximum output delay.',r'$2n+1$',[r'$2n$',r'$n+3$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:2*n+1,[lambda n:2*n,lambda n:n+3,lambda n:n+1],r'The final carry needs $n$ carry stages. The highest sum waits for the carry through the preceding $n-1$ stages and then adds its three-unit sum delay. Its path is one unit longer than the final carry, so it determines the maximum.','Critical-path analysis compares all candidate output paths.',['The final carry is always the slowest adder output.','Delays on parallel paths must all be added together.','Only the number of bits matters, regardless of per-stage delay.'],'Add delays along one dependency path, then take the maximum across output paths. The highest sum and final carry have different final logic. A carry-lookahead architecture or different arrival times would require a different dependency graph rather than the same ripple expression.')
f(t,'rc-scaling','Separating internal and external RC contributions',r'An initial delay has equal internal and fixed-load contributions, each one time unit. Scale transistor widths by $n$: resistance divides by $n$, internal capacitance multiplies by $n$, and the external load stays fixed. Find new delay.',r'$1+1/n$',[r'$2/n$',r'$2$',r'$1$'],lambda n:1+F(1,n),[lambda n:F(2,n),lambda n:2,lambda n:1],r'The internal RC product has opposite width scalings and remains one. The fixed-load product retains its capacitance while resistance falls, giving one over the scale factor. Add the two contributions rather than scaling every capacitance identically.','This simplified model leaves a nonzero internal-delay floor.',['Increasing width drives every delay term to zero.','The external fixed load doubles whenever transistor width doubles.','Internal capacitance is independent of width in the stated model.'],'The internal term cancels the resistance reduction because its capacitance grows. The external term falls because that load is fixed. The asymptotic floor follows from these assumptions; interconnect, input loading and more detailed device effects need their own model.')
f(t,'ring-frequency','Two transitions per oscillation cycle',r'A ring has $n$ inverters, each with propagation delay two nanoseconds, and $n$ is odd. Find the oscillation frequency in MHz under the standard delay model.',r'$250/n$',[r'$500/n$',r'$1000/n$',r'$250n$'],lambda n:F(250,n),[lambda n:F(500,n),lambda n:F(1000,n),lambda n:250*n],r'A transition traverses all inverters in two times the stage-count nanoseconds. A complete cycle needs both a rising and a falling traversal, doubling that time again. Inverting the resulting nanosecond period and converting to MHz gives the stated frequency.','Odd inversion count is essential to the ordinary ring-oscillation model.',['An even-inverter feedback loop has the same oscillation guarantee.','One traversal is always a full cycle.','Frequency and period increase together.'],'The odd number of inversions provides a reversed logical state after a traversal, with delay allowing transitions to circulate. An even inversion loop can settle to a static logical state. The standard period estimate also assumes comparable rise/fall delays.',seeds=(5,7))
f(t,'consensus-cost','Literal cost of independent hazard bridges',r'For each of $n$ independent outputs, use SOP $ab+\overline a c$ and add the consensus term that covers the static-one hazard under a change in $a$. Count literals across all completed SOPs.',r'$6n$',[r'$4n$',r'$2n$',r'$3n$'],lambda n:6*n,[lambda n:4*n,lambda n:2*n,lambda n:3*n],r'When both other inputs are one, the two original product terms can briefly turn off as the complemented and uncomplemented versions of the changing input propagate. The consensus product $bc$ remains true. Three two-literal products now give six literals per independent output.','A redundant term in steady-state logic can be necessary under a delay-aware implementation model.',['Logical redundancy proves that a term is physically useless.','The consensus term changes the stable truth table.','Hazard analysis can ignore which input changes.'],'The consensus theorem preserves the stable function, while common coverage of the transition supplies a continuously asserted product under the single-input-delay model. Multi-input changes and dynamic hazards need different analysis; the given bridge addresses a static-one transition.')
save('original-expanded-computing.json')
